> It is meaningless to call an technique "polynomial time" unless you
> can vary the size of the problem it's solving -- increasing it
> without bound, strictly speaking.  The static vision problem, in
> contrast, involves input from a fixed number of photoreceptors.
> Roughly and informally, a constant-size problem is solved in
> constant time. 

If I stated that mere engineering improvements in the
perception problem cannot possibly generate useful
results, I overstated my case.

But it is certainly true that the combinatorial explosion
means that mere improvements in power applied to existing
methods will not do anything very useful.

I presume that you are claiming that by merely improving
existing algorithms so as to achieve a slower explosion
and throwing enormous increases in computational power at
the problem we will achieve acceptable results, that no
fundamental breakthroughs are needed, merely improvements
in engineering.

It is quite possible you are right, but so far the
prospects for a mere engineering solution are looking very
grim.  No significant progress on this class of problem
has been made since 1980, though there has been progress
in associated fields, which might feed back into this
problem.  But I must confess that poor results are not
proof of impossibility, and if I appeared to claim
otherwise I was wrong.

Also as Price correctly points out, there is as yet no
evidence that quantum computers are useful with this class
of problem.  Indeed, apart from the fact that we can
effortlessly solve this problem there is no evidence that
anything is useful for this class of problem.

(I am hoping that his bet will turn up something useful
for this class of problem.)


> (One could, conversely, define problem size in terms of
> scene complexity.  Larger problems now result in longer
> solution times; it is not clear to me that a quantification
> of the values would show polynomial performance.

However those who claim these magic algorithms have been
notably unsuccessful in constructing programs to parse
scenes into objects in polynomial time. (Polynomial with
respect to scene complexity.)

The most successful scene parser was the origami microworld
parser.  If you can improve on this I am sure the AI
community would be very interested in hearing from you.

> This leads into a simple, even obvious, explanation of how
> brains do complicated things in short times: they're highly
> parallel.

Parallelism is only useful in linear time algorithms, or
for small non polynomial problems at which one throws a
very large number of computational units.

By known algorithms, the perception problem is certainly
not small.  But you could well argue, and I assume that
you are arguing, that mere engineering improvements in the
algorithms will make the problem small enough to be swiftly
solved by computers employing 10^14 units in parallel.

