You are on!


Proposed terms for the bet:


We will each place 5000 in escrow with a suitable third
party, someone with a background in computational quantum
physics, and preferably someone of adequate wealth and
reputation .  We will pay him a small fee for this service.

(Nominations anyone?  Surely we have a computational
quantum physicist on the list?)

I will propose an operation that a quantum computer can
clearly solve in polynomial time, generating interesting
information.

Within three months of the escrow I will write and post a
computer program for a conventional computer (IBM PC
clone, code C++) that emulates the quantum computer
solving this class of operation, for any problem of this
class

Within three months of the escrow, you will write and post
a more efficient program, capable of being executed on an
IBM PC, in C or C++, for which the running time scales
polynomially. This program must produce information from
the given data equivalent to that produced by the quantum
computer, or emulate the quantum computer, or produce
information from which a polynomial time emulation of the
quantum computer can readily be constructed by known and
well established methods such as random sampling of phase
space or linearization.

If I write my program, and you fail to write your program,
or your program takes non polynomial time, or you admit
defeat, I win.

If I fail to write the program, or the escrow holder rules
that my program fails to emulate the quantum computer that
it claims to emulate, or you write a program that does the
job in polynomial time, you win.

The result will be approximations to n real numbers.  The
input data on which the answer depends will be order n
small integers, or equivalently order n log(n) bits.  The
emulated quantum computer will take time proportional to
P(n), where P(n) is a low order polynomial of n.  The
emulating classical computer that I program will take time
proportional (2^n)*P(n), which is non polynomially large.

To win the bet, your program must take time proportional
to a polynomial of n.

Note that the terms of the bet are that the quantum
