> 	
>   Seems simple enough.

We have been here before.  Approximations to the best move
in chess (terminating the search prematurely) are good
moves.  Approximations to the correct parsing of sense data
(terminating the search prematurely) are incorrect
parsings.

It has been tried.  Every perception program that contains
arbitrary search limits gives radically wrong answers when
it misses the right answer, not "almost correct" answers.


> I would also add: 3) biological computation could be a very
> efficient search engine in problem space. It's been
> searching for millions of years. It probably solved many NP
> design problems a long time ago, the results being
> incorporated into our genes.

True.

One form of this theory is the theory that our searches
are efficient because of a multitude of ad hoc special
purpose fixes specific to particular problem domains.

However the special purpose hardware for particular senses
seems to do straightforward stuff.  Perception seems to
involve a general capacity that can be applied to any sense
modality.  For example when you are looking an image that
is hard to make sense of, you will not attend to what you
are hearing.

This does not prove that the theory is false, but so far
that form of the theory has not been fruitfull.

An alternate form of that theory, that I find more
plausible, is that our brains, and the brains of most
animals, contain a small number of powerfull and general
methods for solving large classes of these problems, and
that existing known algorithms are vastly inferior to these
innate algorithms.

