> Notice that creatures with better visual
> capabilities frequently have larger visual, uh, cortices.

Indeed they frequently do, but they sometimes do not. 

Most spiders seem to have visual perception that is not
drastically superior from that of AI program, and the
horseshoe crab has been shown to have visual perception
comparable to that of AI program and subject to the same
kind of limitations as AI programs.  (Which makes it
effectively blind for most purposes, even though its eyes
have reasonable resolution.)

Surprise surprise, visual perception in horseshoe crabs is 
well understood.  There are scientists who say that they 
know how it works on the level of visual circuitry.  These 
same scientists also say that the horseshoe crab is 
effectively blind.

But certain spiders, in particular spiders that hunt
visually, rather than waiting for their prey to come to
them, seem to see the world much as we see it.  I would
conjecture that their categories are much broader and
cruder than our categories, but AI programs suffer from
narrow rigid and inflexible categories, not overly broad
categories.

A pigeon cannot tell a triangle and a square apart.  A
computer cannot tell that they are alike.

A pigeon can tell the difference between a photograph of
a tree from a distance, and a photograph of a vegetable
close up, independently of the particular tree and the
particular vegetable.  A computer cannot form such
categories at all.

A pigeon can learn to distinguish a smiling human face 
from a frowning human face, independently of the 
person whose face it is, though it cannot recognize these 
categories innately as a humans can.  A computer cannot 
tell that a face is a face.

