James A. Donald:

> > Science assumes that the entities it deals with are real,

In <17438@price.demon.co.uk>, price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)  wrote:

> Operationalism is compatible with this.  That's why I described myself
> as an operational realist.  

I did not read that.  From my knowledge of operationalism,
an operational realist is a contradiction in terms, like
"legal realist" or "military intelligence".

Perhaps we are arguing about different topics.

To me, operationalism is closely similar to logical
positivism, though with less philosophical crap, and very
few famous scientists were logical positivists.

Please specify what you mean by operationalism.  If you
merely mean finding truth from experiment and the evidence
of the senses, then the category is meaningless, because
there are no non operationalists outside lunatic asylums.

If you mean that there is no meaningful reality except the
results of experiments and observation, which is what
operationalism normally means, then this is obviously
incompatible with Occams razor.  An aesthetic judgment of
theories is clearly necessary, because there are an
infinite number of theories that can explain any finite set
of facts.

> > Operationalism fails to describe the world, and it fails
> > to describe how scientists actually act in practice.

> Tell that to Newton, Einstein and Heisenberg.  Newton and Einstein I've
> covered earlier in the thread.  From Heisenberg's seminal July 29 1925
> paper on QM (translated in _Sources of Quantum Mechanics_, Dover). 
> The abstract for the article consists of _one_ sentence:
> 
>      "The present paper seeks to establish a basis for theoretical
>      quantum mechanics founded exclusively upon relationships
>      between quantities which in principle are observable."

Heisenberg is using the word "observable" in a sense quite
different to that used by the operationalists. 
Operationalists who quote him are mangling quantum
mechanics just as much as those Eastern mystics are.

Note the words "*in principle* observable".  Especially
note those words "in principle".  Among the many
observables that Heisenberg treats as real things is the
local EM potential, (not merely the local EM field, which
is indeed locally observable, but the local EM potential
which is not in fact locally observable.

To Heisenberg things that can be observed "in principle"
are observables, even if they can never be observed in
practice.  Anything that could affect the world is an
observable, even if we have no way of discovering the
effect, and if we were to discover the effect there would
be no way of determining the cause.

Heisenberg was indeed an operationalist in certain
important regards, but the philosophy called operationalism
has been crudely retrofitted onto his interpretation of
quantum mechanics.  Newton, Einstein, and the like
certainly were not operationalists, and it would have been
inappropriate for them to have been in their fields of
study.

Heisenberg talked about what is observable *in principle*.
Operationalists talk about what we in fact observe in
actual experiments.  These are very different things.

Heisenberg is using the word observable in a special sense,
a sense appropriate to quantum mechanics.  Those
who use observable in a different sense, the more usual
sense, and claim the support of Heisenberg, speak bunkum.

Obviously any true statement must imply facts about the
world and our relationship to the world.  Nobody disagrees
with this, and in that sense everyone is an operationalist.
The dispute arises over the false claim that certain facts
are not facts about the world, that certain issues do not
have consequences for the way the world works, when in fact
they do.  Classic example is of course Occams razor, which
is an aesthetic criterion.  If you agree that aesthetic
criteria and such like provide us with meaningful
knowledge of the world, and presumably from your discussion
of General Relativity you do believe this, then who is that
who is not an operationalist by your definition of
operationalist?

Even religious mystics are operationalists by this
definition, they merely err in that they use irrelevant
and misleading evidence, for example ancient books of
dubious authorship, and visions that we would reasonably
suspect of being hallucinations.

The communists are certainly operationalists by this
definition, since they, (very reluctantly) put their
theory to the test.

Who is that is not an operationalist by your definition of
operationalist?

I repeat the dispute arises not from the issue that truths
must be testable against the world.  Everyone agrees on
this except straw men of your own imagination.

The dispute arises from the question of what truths are
testable against the world, an it is on this issue that those 
whom I call operationalists are incorrect.

