understanding each other more.

> My original question was about MCP's use of the word 'meaningless.'
> He seemed to think that because a certain Randian statement
> ('existence exists') is wooly and non-operational, that it was also
> therefore 'meaningless.' I responded because I thought this was a bit
> too much of a jump. I think Randians understand each other,

I'm not convinced that all Randians have a similar understanding of it. 
At once point I thought it was synonymous with 'reality exists', which
I accept as a tautology or definition of reality [reality = that which
exists], but I have since been told that Rand distinguished the two
statements - unfortunately my source couldn't remember the distinction!

Perhaps it is time to examine the statement 'existence exists' from
another angle.  Consider the negation of 'existence exists', or as I
prefer 'reality exists', which would be 'reality doesn't exist'.  There
are lots of people who would superficially agree with this statement -
for instance some Hindus believe the world is an illusion in the mind
of Vishnu, the preserver (if this isn't true, substitute Hindu for your
favourite flaky religion/belief/quantum philosophy/mumbo-jumbo).  But
what do they mean by 'reality' in this case?  That reality is subsumed
and contained within Vishnu's mind.  When they hear us use the word
'reality' they are assuming that we mean the world as it appears to our
senses.  What they mean by reality is the 'higher' reality of
Vishnu/Brahma/Shiva.  Thus they are not denying that 'reality exists'
but rather that 'reality is as we perceive'.  It would seem that
negating 'reality exists' is fruitless.  Upon examination everyone
accepts that reality exists, so the reaction has to be, so what?

Dave correctly summarises my position as:

> He apparently thinks that in some kinds of talk, even when both
> listener and (sometimes) audience think they understand each other,
> there is really an absence of meaning;

Here an example:  A while back on the list there was some discussion
about rights (well, there often is).  Perry would often get into an
argument with someone who would say "I think strangling kittens is
morally wrong" or whatever.  Whereupon Perry would challenge them to
define what they meant by 'right' and 'wrong'.  Can't remember anyone
ever being able to coherently rise to the challenge, because the concept
of absolute right and wrong is not capable of an operational definition.

What experiment can you perform to test whether eating fish on Fridays
is a sin?  Yet many people believe in absolute right and wrong and talk
to each other about it (at great length).

> but in other cases, even though the talk is rather ordinary and not
> self-consciously operationalized, that there is meaning;

Whereas by viewing rights and wrongs as concepts that arise from
interactions between people, and perhaps as Schelling points, PPL and
customary law we can talk theorise about these things, and actually get
somewhere.  For instance the old arguments about copyrights, patents and
intellectual property rights more or less died out when we realised that
we can leave competing protection agencies to 'slug it out' in the
market place and see which predominates.

> and that operationalism defines the line between these two kinds 
> of talk.  I don't understand how this line is drawn.

I hope the two example from the list have been helpful.

> Here is my test for meaning. Take two persons who think they
> understand each other. Have them each separately talk to a third
> person until the test subject is satisfied that the other understands
> the topic. Then ask the third person whether the first two understand
> each other (not whether he agrees with them). 

This procedure has much merit.  I like it.

> Perhaps this is too subjective to rate the title operational. Also,
> it's rather trivial, since only lunatics would ever fail it.

And not even then.  See "Can You Speak Venusian?" or somesuch by Patrick
Moore.  Patrick found two lunatics who were convinced they could speak
Venusian (click bang whistle etc) and introduced them to each other (on
TV, I think), expecting denunciations and confusion.  Whereupon they
chatted merrily away, convinced they understood each other.  Certainly
the "mutual comprehensibility test" is a necessity for meaningfulness,
but I doubt whether it is sufficient.

> It is obviously distinct from MCP's test, although I don't know what
> that is.

Doesn't sound to different from the experiment with the physics students
we talked about, where the test was whether they made sense to the
experimenter directly.

> I'm claiming that by your criteria, "novel" and "Picasso's Blue
> period" are meaningless. If you think they are defined operationally,
> I don't understand at all.

I don't understand the problem with "novel (book)".  Due you mean the
subjective evaluation about a novel's content?  When is a novel not a
novel?  Subjective opinions are fine and admissible as evidence. 
Science works by consensus.  If by "Picasso's Blue period" you mean how
do we verify historical events, I guess the answer is that we can only
verify the records left behind, check that they're consistent etc (but
I'm not sure if this is what you mean) and form probabilistic
impressions.  Or is it a query about art in general?  If it I suppose
I would say that art is not concerned about gathering and classifying
knowledge scientifically, so the scientific methodology is fairly
irrelevant.  Could be wrong, though, art, ironically, has always seemed
meaningless to me.  Wouldn't surprise me if it is all just meaningless
mumbo-jumbo.  Reminds of something I heard attributed to Dirac about
poetry.  "Poetry obscures what physics makes clear."  (not verbatim, but
general gist)

So you could be correct that art is non-operational (I really can't say)
and I could be right that it is meaningless.  Should that disturb us? 
Well, it doesn't disturb me.  Guess I'm a philistine, eh?

> So [Newton's] position on the qualities of bodies is clear. How 
> about philosophy and theology?

His premises (a belief in the bible and the wisdom of the Ancients)
being flawed meant that his conclusions (Arianism, astrology and
alchemy) were flawed.

> Newton's formulation of physics certainly was better than his
> predecessors', but that doesn't mean theirs was meaningless.

Granted.

> ... it's not obvious to me how to apply operationalism to the 
> question of identity through change, etc.

Me neither, it's a tough nut to crack.  We'd have to start by defining
a test for identity.  I can't think of one that I'm completely happy
with.

> Dave Burns

